The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions and statements concerning Somalia, in particular resolutions 2713 (2023), 2762 (2024) and 2776 (2025), and resolution 2664 (2022),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity, political independence and unity of Somalia, and to the principles of non-interference, good-neighbourliness and regional cooperation,

Recognizing the primary responsibility of the Federal Government of Somalia for peace, security and stability in the country, and stressing the need for continued support from the African Union, the United Nations and international partners,

Welcoming the deployment of the African Union Support and Stabilization Mission in Somalia (AUSSOM) and its efforts to support the Federal Government of Somalia, in close coordination with the United Nations and international partners,

Expressing grave concern at the ongoing threat posed by Al-Shabaab and Islamic State in Somalia (IS-Somalia), including their attacks on civilians, security forces and regional States, and their destabilizing activities,

Expressing concern at the continued flow of weapons and ammunition to Al-Shabaab and IS-Somalia, including from Yemen and through maritime routes, and at reports of illegal arms transfers and diversion, which threaten peace and security,

Recognizing the need to ensure that sanctions are targeted and do not impede humanitarian assistance, and recalling resolution 2664 (2022),

Stressing the importance of the full, equal and meaningful participation of women in peace and security, and of youth, and emphasizing the need for accountability for violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law,

Welcoming the continued efforts of the Panel of Experts and the Sanctions Committee, and emphasizing the importance of cooperation with regional organizations and Member States,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend, until 15 December 2026, the measures set out in resolution 2713 (2023), as extended by resolution 2776 (2025), including the arms embargo on Al-Shabaab and other armed groups, the travel ban, and the asset freeze;

2. Decides to extend the mandate of the Panel of Experts established by resolution 2444 (2018) and extended by resolution 2713 (2023), as extended by resolution 2776 (2025), until 15 January 2027, and requests the Panel to provide a midterm report by 15 July 2026 and a final report by 15 November 2026, and to submit updates every 90 days;

3. Reaffirms the provisions of resolution 2664 (2022) and decides that the humanitarian exemption applies to the sanctions regime renewed by this resolution, and requests the Committee to ensure that humanitarian assistance is not hindered;

4. Calls upon all Member States to fully implement the arms embargo, including by preventing the sale, supply or transfer of weapons and ammunition to Al-Shabaab and IS-Somalia, and by inspecting suspicious vessels and cargo in their territory and maritime spaces, consistent with international law;

5. Urges the Federal Government of Somalia to strengthen its arms and ammunition management, including by notifying the Committee of all transfers and ensuring proper record-keeping, in order to prevent diversion to terrorist groups;

6. Expresses concern at the threat posed by weapons flows from Yemen, and requests the Panel of Experts, in coordination with the Panel of Experts established by resolution 2140 (2014), to investigate and report on such flows and on possible links between Al-Shabaab and Houthi actors, and to recommend measures to address them;

7. Condemns all terrorist attacks by Al-Shabaab and IS-Somalia, and calls upon Member States to counter terrorist financing, recruitment and propaganda, including by sharing information and strengthening border and maritime security;

8. Reaffirms the sovereignty, territorial integrity and political independence of Somalia, and calls upon all States in the region to respect these principles, refrain from actions that may undermine stability, and resolve disputes through peaceful dialogue;

9. Requests AUSSOM, within its mandate and capabilities, to monitor and report on violations of the arms embargo, in coordination with the Panel of Experts and the Federal Government of Somalia;

10. Urges the Committee to consider additional designations of individuals and entities engaged in illegal arms trafficking, financing or supporting Al-Shabaab and IS-Somalia;

11. Requests the Secretary-General to ensure that the United Nations provides appropriate support to the Panel of Experts, and requests the Panel to keep the Council informed of its findings;

12. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

