Welcoming the continued efforts of the Panel of Experts and the Sanctions Committee, and emphasizing the importance of cooperation with regional organizations and Member States,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend, until 15 December 2026, the measures set out in resolution 2713 (2023), as extended by resolution 2776 (2025), including the arms embargo on Al-Shabaab, IS-Somalia and other individuals and entities designated by the Committee, the travel ban, and the asset freeze; and reaffirms that the arms embargo does not apply to weapons, ammunition and military equipment supplied to the Federal Government of Somalia or AUSSOM, in accordance with the notification procedures of resolution 2713 (2023);

2. Decides to extend the mandate of the Panel of Experts established by resolution 2444 (2018) and extended by resolution 2713 (2023), as extended by resolution 2776 (2025), until 15 January 2027, and requests the Panel to provide a midterm report by 15 July 2026 and a final report by 15 November 2026, and to submit written updates every 90 days beginning 90 days after the adoption of this resolution; further requests the Panel to include in its reports information on violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law, including sexual and gender-based violence and the recruitment and use of children, to integrate a gender perspective into its work, and to assess the humanitarian impact of the measures and any obstacles to the delivery of humanitarian assistance;

3. Reaffirms the provisions of resolution 2664 (2022) and decides that the humanitarian exemption applies to the sanctions regime renewed by this resolution, and requests the Committee to ensure that humanitarian assistance is not hindered and that humanitarian actors and their activities are not subject to unnecessary restrictions, over-compliance or administrative impediments;

4. Calls upon all Member States to fully implement the arms embargo, including by preventing the sale, supply or transfer of weapons and ammunition to Al-Shabaab and IS-Somalia, and by inspecting suspicious vessels and cargo in their territory and maritime spaces, consistent with international law, including the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea, and with full respect for the sovereignty and jurisdiction of coastal States and the rights and obligations of flag States; and calls upon Member States to share information on suspicious maritime activity with the Committee and the Panel of Experts;

5. Urges the Federal Government of Somalia, with the support of international partners, to strengthen its arms and ammunition management, including by notifying the Committee, in accordance with existing procedures under resolution 2713 (2023), of transfers to its security forces, and ensuring proper record-keeping, in order to prevent diversion to terrorist groups; and encourages Member States and international partners to provide capacity-building support to the Federal Government of Somalia in this regard;

6. Expresses concern at the threat posed by weapons flows from Yemen, including by sea through the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden, and at reports of possible links between Al-Shabaab and Houthi actors and other relevant armed actors in Yemen, including financial, logistical, recruitment and weapons-smuggling links; requests the Panel of Experts, in coordination with the Panel of Experts established by resolution 2140 (2014) and relevant international and regional mechanisms, including the International Maritime Organization and relevant maritime security entities, to investigate and report, within existing mandates and resources and on the basis of credible evidence, without prejudging responsibility, on such flows and links, and to recommend, as appropriate, measures consistent with resolution 2140 (2014) and additional designation targets to the Committee; and calls upon all Member States to cooperate fully with the Panel, including by sharing information on such flows and links;

7. Condemns all terrorist attacks by Al-Shabaab and IS-Somalia, and calls upon Member States to counter terrorist financing, recruitment and propaganda, including by sharing information and strengthening border and maritime security; and calls for safe, timely and unhindered humanitarian access to all persons in need in Somalia, in accordance with international humanitarian law;

8. Calls upon all parties to respect international humanitarian law and human rights law, ensure the protection of civilians, and allow and facilitate safe, rapid and unhindered humanitarian access to all persons in need; stresses that counter-terrorism measures must comply with international law;

9. Reaffirms the sovereignty, territorial integrity, political independence and unity of Somalia, including over all its land, sea, airspace and maritime territory, and calls upon all States, in particular those in the region, to respect these principles, refrain from any action that may undermine the stability or territorial integrity of Somalia, including unilateral arrangements or the conclusion or implementation of bilateral agreements or arrangements affecting Somali territory without the consent of the Federal Government of Somalia, and to resolve disputes through peaceful dialogue and regional mechanisms, in accordance with international law, including the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea;

10. Stresses that the deployment of AUSSOM and all international support to Somalia must be in full respect of Somalia's sovereignty, territorial integrity and political independence, and reaffirms that AUSSOM shall operate with the full consent and ownership of the Federal Government of Somalia;

11. Requests AUSSOM, within its mandate and capabilities, to monitor and report on violations of the arms embargo, including illicit maritime transfers, and to report on violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law, including conflict-related sexual violence, in coordination with the Panel of Experts, the Federal Government of Somalia, the African Union and relevant maritime security operations, without prejudice to the mandate of AUSSOM;

12. Encourages Member States and relevant international and regional organizations to provide capacity-building assistance to Somalia and regional States, upon request, to strengthen maritime security, border management and arms and ammunition management, in full respect of Somali ownership and sovereignty;

13. Urges the Committee to consider, as a matter of priority and on the basis of credible evidence and in accordance with the Committee's guidelines and fair and clear procedures, additional designations of individuals and entities engaged in illegal arms trafficking, financing or supporting Al-Shabaab and IS-Somalia, including those involved in Houthi-Al-Shabaab cooperation;

14. Requests the Committee, in consultation with the Federal Government of Somalia and the African Union, to review by 30 June 2026 the arms embargo and related inspection and notification measures, with a view to considering appropriate adjustments, including easing or lifting where conditions permit, and to report to the Council;

15. Requests the Committee, with the support of the Panel of Experts and in consultation with the Federal Government of Somalia, to conduct a comprehensive assessment of the Al-Shabaab sanctions regime, including its impact on the Federal Government's capacity to combat Al-Shabaab and on humanitarian access, and to report to the Council by 15 July 2026 with recommendations on adjusting the regime as necessary;

16. Requests the Secretary-General to ensure that the United Nations provides appropriate support to the Panel of Experts, and requests the Panel to keep the Council informed of its findings, including on humanitarian access and on the impact of the measures on women and children;

17. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

